  message: "P0 closed by Phase 6 PR3 (commit ea0154e54f). Self-analysis Symbol.stable_id collision rate 60.2% → 0.8% (well below the 10% target). Top group (155 zero-param bash functions in scripts/lib/forgejo-api.sh sharing one stable_id) now distinguished. Validator's _check_cross_field_coherence has a new collision-umbrella check that emits one ValidationViolation when the rate exceeds 5%; on the fixed corpus the umbrella is dormant. Residual 0.8% lives in WI-bupim. Needs bakeoff validation."  # ec0f
- op: discuss  # 1d98
  at: '2026-06-02T20:44:57Z'  # 1d98
  by: agent  # 1d98
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 1d98
  clock: 9  # 1d98
  nonce: 1d98  # 1d98
  message: "Pass 36 (continuing 06022026_1622) substrate-size-dependent collision rate batch (2 findings).\n\nF36.B4: --max-files 100 substrate (3061 symbols, ~91% smaller than DEFAULT 34253) produces stable_id collision rate 5.9% (180/3061 symbols), exceeding INV-bazij 5% threshold and triggering cross_field validator. Top colliding groups: SQL parse artifacts ('SELECT users' 13×), kafka-linker placeholders ('kafka:publish:topic' 8×, 'kafka:publish:x' 7×). These are exactly WI-bupim residual scope (typed-tier + linker call_site).\n\nF36.B5: --max-file-bytes 1024 substrate (242 symbols, ~99% smaller than DEFAULT) produces collision rate 14.0% (34/242 symbols), 2.8× the threshold. Top groups are bash export-variable repetitions: FORGEJO_TOKEN 5×, CI_TIMEOUT_SECONDS 4×, 'usage' 4×. Exact pass-24 F24.6 WI-bupim 'bash analyzer treats each export VAR statement as a distinct Symbol' pattern at extreme manifestation.\n\nSubstrate-size vs collision-rate curve (sub-axis of INV-bazij):\n  - 34253 nodes (DEFAULT)        : 0.84%   ✓ under\n  - 4959  nodes (tracker-pkg)    : 0.81%   ✓ under\n  - 3061  nodes (--max-files 100): 5.9%    ✗ over\n  - 242   nodes (--max-file-bytes 1024): 14.0%  ✗ over\n\nNEW pattern: INV-bazij 5% threshold is calibrated for production-scale substrates; size-limited cuts trip it because hash-collision-producing symbols (SQL parse artifacts, kafka placeholders, bash export VARs) don't shrink proportionally with file count. Implication for validator: threshold could be expressed as 'collisions per K nodes' or 'collisions excluding linker-synth-placeholder cohort' rather than absolute percentage. WI-bupim residual quantified at extreme cuts; absence of mitigation at the linker side (kafka:publish:* uses literal string as last id segment, bash export VAR not deduped) is the underlying cause."  # 1d98
- op: discuss  # 0981
  at: '2026-06-02T21:28:08Z'  # 0981
  by: agent  # 0981
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 0981
  clock: 10  # 0981
  nonce: '0981'  # 0981
  message: "Pass 37 (continuing 06022026_1700) collision-rate curve quantification (3 findings).\n\nF37.A1: collision_rate(N) is monotone-decreasing on both --max-files and --max-file-bytes sweeps. 5% threshold cross-over:\n  --max-files: between N=100 (5.9%) and N=500 (1.36%) — cross-over ~120-200 files\n  --max-file-bytes: between 4096 (6.13%) and 16384 (1.08%) — cross-over ~4K-16K bytes\nINV-bazij threshold calibrated for codebases producing ≥500 files or ≥16KB-files; smaller substrates exceed threshold 2-4×.\n\nF37.A2: collision COUNT is near-constant under --max-files (178/180/180/263/289 across N=10/50/100/500/1000) but scales-with-size under --max-file-bytes (34/34/59/130/227/289). Different pruning modes prune different colliding cohorts:\n  --max-files keeps small files (configs/scripts) where SQL parse artifacts ('SELECT users') + kafka-linker placeholders concentrate — colliding cohort is sticky-by-being-in-small-files\n  --max-file-bytes prunes EVERYTHING above N bytes; bash export-VAR pattern dominates at extreme cuts then thins as byte-budget grows\nThese are two distinct WI-bupim sub-residuals; fix-paths differ (file-by-file dedup vs across-file canonicalization).\n\nF37.A3: at N=10 files (893 nodes, 757 symbols) the collision rate is 23.51% — 4.7× INV-bazij threshold. Highest collision rate observed across all 36 dogfooding passes. Useful as a stress-test baseline; can serve as a CI fixture for INV-bazij validator-threshold-on-extreme-substrates testing.\n\nFull curve table:\n  --max-files: N=10 → 23.51%, N=50 → 8.54%, N=100 → 5.9%, N=500 → 1.36%, N=1000+ → 0.85%\n  --max-file-bytes: N=512 → 17.99%, N=1024 → 14.0%, N=4096 → 6.13%, N=16384 → 1.08%, N=65536 → 1.02%, ∞ → 0.85%\nAligns with pass-36 F36.B4+B5 (5.9% at N=100 / 14.0% at byte=1024) and extends to 7 additional data points."  # 0981
- op: discuss  # dceb
  at: '2026-06-02T22:15:31Z'  # dceb
  by: agent  # dceb
  actor: jgstern_agent  # dceb
  clock: 11  # dceb
  nonce: dceb  # dceb
  message: "Pass 38 (2026-06-02, dogfooding) — 4 findings batched (INV-bazij scope extension):\n\nF38.B1: Validator denominator excludes None stable_ids. Validator at --max-files=10 reports 178/757 (23.5%); raw probe shows 178/893 (19.93%). The 136-symbol delta has stable_id=None (linker-synthesized placeholders). INV-bazij 5% threshold calibrated against bearing-symbol denominator only. Documentation gap: no per-axis denominator note in spec; consumer reading 'collision rate' may compute differently.\n\nF38.B6: ALL 54 colliding stable_ids at --max-files=10 ALSO collide in ROOT default substrate (54 ⊂ 95 ROOT colliders). Truncation SURFACES collisions, does not introduce them. The pass-36 F36.B4+B5 collision-rate(N) curve and F37.A1-A3 cross-over observations describe REVEALING the underlying collision substrate; the colliding hash inputs are stable across substrate slicing. Truncation amplifies measurement but doesn't change the bug surface.\n\nF38.B7: 41 ROOT-default colliders NOT present in max10 (the source files weren't analyzed at --max-files=10). Tells us collision detection is path-stratifiable; mathematically the rate(N) curve is monotone-non-decreasing as N shrinks BECAUSE colliders persist and bearing-symbol count shrinks faster.\n\nF38.D3: scripts/ DEFAULT mode produces 1 INV-bazij collision-rate violation at 7.0% (29/416) — FIRST non-truncated DEFAULT substrate to breach 5% threshold. Top groups: FORGEJO_TOKEN 5×, CI_TIMEOUT_SECONDS 4×, usage 4× — all bash export-VAR cohort. INV-bazij threshold-calibration assumption (≥500 files / production scale; pass-37 F37.A1) doesn't hold for bash-heavy codebases of any size. Cross-substrate INV-bazij data points now: ROOT (34253) 0.84%, TRACKER (4959) 0.81%, SCRIPTS (416) 7.0% DEFAULT-BREACH, CLI_PKG (32) 0%, --max-files 100 5.9%, --max-file-bytes 1024 14%, --max-files 10 23.5%. Substrate-size NOT sole determinant — codebase composition (bash-heavy vs pure-Python) is the bigger driver."  # dceb
- op: discuss  # b328
  at: '2026-06-02T22:52:36Z'  # b328
  by: agent  # b328
  actor: jgstern_agent  # b328
  clock: 12  # b328
  nonce: b328  # b328
  message: "Pass 39 (2026-06-02, dogfooding) — F39.C2 SECOND DEFAULT-mode breach + TS hash-input cohort cross-substrate verification:\n\nF39.C2: HTRAC produces 1 INV-bazij violation at 16.88% (13/77) on DEFAULT mode — SECOND non-truncated DEFAULT substrate to breach 5% threshold (after pass-38 F38.D3 SCRIPTS at 7.0%). All 13 colliding nodes are exactly the 4 TS hash-input bug cohorts identified in pass-38 F38.B4:\n- sha256:f4a7af6e179e8aba — {connect, connectWebSocket, registerServiceWorker, scheduleReconnect}\n- sha256:5b1252b57e00fd12 — {ItemDetail.render, ItemList.render, TrackerApp.render}\n- sha256:465a58dbfa932b3a — {ItemDetail.onBack, ItemDetail.onDiscussSubmit, TrackerApp.connectedCallback, TrackerApp.onBack}\n- sha256:e067b2ec87edc43c — {ItemDetail.onDiscussInput, ItemDetail.onStatusChange}\n\nCROSS-SUBSTRATE CONFIRMATION: identical 4 stable_ids collide across (1) pass-38 max10 (--max-files=10 root truncation), (2) pass-38 ROOT default, (3) pass-39 HTRAC default. Production-scale bug verified on 3 independent substrates. The bug is in js_ts.py's stable_id hash computation (excludes name/path/class disambiguation when computing stable_id for TS function + method symbols).\n\nUpdated cross-substrate INV-bazij data points:\n- ROOT (34253) → 0.84%\n- TRACKER (4959) → 0.81%\n- HTRAC (77) → 16.88% DEFAULT-BREACH ← NEW pass-39\n- SCRIPTS (416) → 7.0% DEFAULT-BREACH (pass-38 F38.D3)\n- CLI_PKG (32) → 0.00%\n- --max-files 100 (3061) → 5.9%\n- --max-file-bytes 1024 (242) → 14.0%\n- --max-files 10 (757) → 23.5%\n\nThree independent codebase-shapes (bash-heavy / Python-pure / TS-pure / CLI-pure) produce 7%-17%-0%-0% collision rates at small scale. Substrate-size is NOT the sole determinant; analyzer-specific stable_id hash bugs dominate at small codebase scale. ROOT 0.84% INCLUDES all these analyzer-specific cohorts as residual; they're masked by the large denominator. WI-bupim multi-mechanism decomposition (per pass-38 F38.B2-B5) confirmed cross-codebase: each cohort surfaces wherever its source analyzer is the dominant scope contributor."  # b328
- op: discuss  # fea8
  at: '2026-06-03T00:13:43Z'  # fea8
  by: agent  # fea8
  actor: jgstern_agent  # fea8
  clock: 13  # fea8
  nonce: fea8  # fea8
  message: "Pass-40 F40.C3 — INV-bazij 6th mechanism (within-file duplicate-heading markdown collisions) + 6-codebase matrix.\n\nDOCS substrate: 11/2861 = 0.38% collision rate. All 4 distinct colliders are markdown sections/links from ONE file: adr/0013-structured-tracker.md. The colliding stable_ids all share format markdown:<path>:1411-1411:<heading>:link — the same line-range across all collisions.\n\nMechanism: The markdown analyzer emits N copies of an 'internal-link' node when the source file has N occurrences of identical heading text. Specifically, adr/0013-structured-tracker.md has '## Key Design Decisions' appearing 4 times (in different sub-sections of the ADR), and each occurrence emits an identical-stable_id link node. Same for 'Compile Rules' (3 and 2 occurrences).\n\nThis is the 6th distinct INV-bazij mechanism, distinct from prior cohorts:\n- SQL parse-artifact ('SELECT users' 13×)\n- kafka-linker placeholder ('kafka:publish:*' 5-8× each)\n- bash export-VAR intrinsic (FORGEJO_TOKEN/CI_TIMEOUT_SECONDS within-name repetition)\n- HTTP-linker URL-variant collapse\n- TypeScript hash-input-insufficient cross-name (4 stable_ids collapse {connect, connectWebSocket, registerServiceWorker, scheduleReconnect})\n- **NEW: markdown within-file duplicate-heading** (multiple ## same-text occurrences in one file)\n\nUpdated 6-codebase INV-bazij matrix:\n| codebase | nodes | rate | dominant cohort |\n| ROOT default | 34253 | 0.84% | mixed |\n| TRACKER | 4959 | 0.81% | python residual |\n| SCRIPTS default | 416 | 7.0% **BREACH** | bash export-VAR intrinsic |\n| HTRAC default | 77 | 16.88% **BREACH** | TS hash-input-insufficient |\n| CLI_PKG | 32 | 0.00% | (nothing) |\n| **DOCS default | 2861 | 0.38% | markdown within-file-dup-heading (NEW)** |\n\nDOCS rate is below 5% threshold so no INV-bazij breach, but the within-file-dup-heading cohort is a NEW mechanism adding to WI-bupim multi-mechanism scope. Composition: markdown analyzer stable_id hash for internal-link kind excludes occurrence-index in same-line-range case. Fix-path: include heading-occurrence index in stable_id hash for markdown internal-link kind.\n\n6-codebase synthesis: codebase composition dominates over codebase SIZE as INV-bazij driver. Three different compositions (bash-heavy / TS-pure / markdown-pure) at small/medium sizes produce 7% / 17% / 0.38% — analyzer-specific hash bugs DOMINATE; ROOT 0.84% MASKS all these cohorts via large denominator."  # fea8
